In a recent conversation, James inquired about handling missing values in a Julia array and element-wise multiplication between a vector and a matrix. He encountered errors with functions like `maximum` and `last` when using `skipmissing`, which prompted suggestions to convert `Base.SkipMissing` types into arrays. I provided example code for both tasks: first, using loops and `mapslices` to extract the last non-missing values from each column of a matrix, ensuring proper conversion to avoid errors. Secondly, I demonstrated how to perform element-wise multiplication of a length \( N \) vector by an \( N \times T \) matrix using broadcasting in Julia. The conversation emphasized efficient coding practices for handling data with missing values and performing mathematical operations in Julia.
